# Valgrind memcheck suppressions for ct-ctgrind — reviewed, not blanket.
#
# The constant-time sign is branch-free: `sign_prehashed_ct_with_k`,
# `derive_nonce_rfc6979_ct`, `affine_x_ct`, and the RCB ladder fold every
# secret-derived decision (key/nonce range, `r`/`s == 0`, the RCB
# identity, `k⁻¹`) into a `subtle::Choice` returned once at the end, so
# they carry NO suppression — any `Cond`/`Value`/`Addr` on tainted data
# in those frames fails the gate. Secret scalars serialize through the
# branch-free `to_be_ct`; the variable-time `to_be` is `#[inline(never)]`
# so misusing it on a secret surfaces at its own (unsuppressed) frame,
# which the `nct_fix__neg__vartime_serialize` control proves the gate
# catches.
#
# Exactly ONE secret-dependent branch is declassified — RFC 6979's
# rejection loop — plus data-oblivious byte plumbing. `Addr` errors (a
# secret-dependent memory *address*, the cache/table-lookup attack
# surface) are NEVER suppressed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# (1) RFC 6979 rejection-loop count (declassification).
#
# `rfc6979_nonce` loops until a candidate lands in `[1, n)`; the loop-exit
# branch depends on the (secret-derived) candidate. This is RFC 6979's
# inherent, irreducible timing signal: reject probability ~2^-32 for
# these curves, effectively always one iteration, revealing negligible
# information about the key. It is the frame's SOLE secret-dependent
# branch — the candidate range test is constant-time (`ct_lt`/
# `ct_is_zero`) and the HMAC-DRBG it drives is data-oblivious (SHA-2/HMAC
# branch on neither key nor message).
{
   rfc6979-rejection-loop-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*rfc6979_nonce*
}
{
   rfc6979-rejection-loop-value
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:*rfc6979_nonce*
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# (2) memcpy / memmove of tainted bytes (data-oblivious).
#
# Parsing the secret scalars and shuffling secret-derived bytes calls
# memcpy/memmove on tainted memory. glibc's memcpy is data-oblivious (it
# copies regardless of value), but Valgrind's shadow propagation through
# its word-at-a-time SIMD paths is imprecise and reports spurious errors
# *inside* the routine. memcpy never branches on the data it copies; a
# secret-dependent *address* would appear a frame above, not here.
{
   memcpy-tainted-from-sign-fixture-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*memcpy*
   fun:ct_fix__ecdsa_sign_*
}
{
   memcpy-tainted-from-sign-fixture-value
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:*memcpy*
   fun:ct_fix__ecdsa_sign_*
}
{
   memcpy-tainted-from-hmac-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*memcpy*
   fun:*hmac_into*
}
{
   memcpy-tainted-from-hmac-value
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:*memcpy*
   fun:*hmac_into*
}
